       At the kernel level, user IDs and group IDs are a per‐thread attribute.
       However,  POSIX  requires  that all threads in a process share the same
       credentials.  The NPTL threading implementation handles the  POSIX  re‐
       quirements  by providing wrapper functions for the various system calls
       that change process UIDs and GIDs.  These wrapper functions  (including
       those  for sseettrreessuuiidd() and sseettrreessggiidd()) employ a signal‐based technique
       to ensure that when one thread changes credentials, all  of  the  other
       threads in the process also change their credentials.  For details, see
       nnppttll(7).

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       POSIX.1‐2024.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       POSIX.1‐2024.  Linux 2.1.44, glibc 2.3.2.  HP‐UX, FreeBSD.

       The  original  Linux sseettrreessuuiidd() and sseettrreessggiidd() system calls supported
       only 16‐bit user and group IDs.  Subsequently, Linux 2.4  added  sseettrree‐‐
       ssuuiidd3322()  and  sseettrreessggiidd3322(),  supporting 32‐bit IDs.  The glibc sseettrree‐‐
       ssuuiidd() and sseettrreessggiidd() wrapper functions transparently  deal  with  the
       variations across kernel versions.

